Superficial retained foreign body (wooden splinter) from gardening to palm
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Visible subepidermal linear sliver, Exquisite focal pressure pain, Unprotected botanical handling mechanism

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-037
Reason for Visit: Acute, exquisitely painful embedded object in the palm requiring urgent extraction to restore hand function.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an embedded wooden splinter in the dominant palm causing severe pain and functional limitation. Urgent clinical evaluation was necessary to perform sterile extraction of organic matter, evaluate for deep palmar space penetration, and prevent aggressive bacterial tenosynovitis before daytime clinic availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I was doing yard work without gloves and got a large piece of wood driven deep into my palm; I cannot hold anything.
History of Present Illness: Patient provides a history of unprotected hand contact with raw wood and gardening materials 3 hours ago. This resulted in focal, sharply localized pain in the palmar skin exacerbated significantly by direct pressure or object manipulation. There is a visible thin, linear sliver of dark material lying obliquely beneath the translucent stratum corneum. The patient notes an inability to comfortably grip objects due to the exquisite focal pressure pain.
Functional Status: Avoiding all active grip functions with the dominant hand.
Social/Safety Concerns: Gardening without appropriate protective equipment (leather gloves).

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 122/76 | HR: 74 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 14 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing, holding the affected hand open and protected.
Physical Exam: Palmar examination reveals a tiny entry portal with minor disruption of the overlying epidermal ridges. A visible subepidermal linear sliver is present. There is very minimal localized erythema strictly bordered around the embedded object. Total absence of active bleeding, purulent discharge, or spreading cellulitis. Normal active and passive range of motion of the hand and digits is observed. Intact deep neurovascular structures are confirmed with completely normal two-point discrimination over the volar pads. The patient has a preserved ability to form a fist, albeit with localized guarding over the splinter site.
Environmental Observations: Gardening debris identified near the back door; no immediate indoor hazard.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Superficial retained foreign body (wooden splinter) to palm - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Flexor tenosynovitis - Ruled out due to: Absence of Kanavel's signs (no fusiform swelling, no pain on passive stretch, no severe flexor resting posture).

Deep palmar space infection - Ruled out due to: Acute timeline with complete lack of severe central palmar fluctuance, dorsal edema, or systemic fever.

Digital nerve puncture - Ruled out due to: Completely normal and symmetrical two-point discrimination distal to the entry portal.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

Organic foreign bodies, particularly wood from gardening, carry a very high risk of introducing fungal and bacterial pathogens (e.g., Sporothrix, Staph aureus) into the fascial planes of the hand. Bedside evaluation definitively localized the splinter to the superficial subepidermal layer without breaching the deep flexor sheaths. Urgent after-hours intervention was clinically mandated to perform precise, sterile mechanical extraction of the splinter before localized edema obscured the fragment, instantly resolving the functional deficit and preventing a delayed, severe hand infection.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered localized superficial anesthesia. Used a sterile large-bore needle and fine forceps to unroof the epidermis and extract the wooden splinter intact. Wound irrigated with sterile saline and dressed with a simple adhesive strip. Tetanus status updated.
Positive Behavioral Change: Keep the tiny extraction site clean and dry for 24 hours. Allowing the small epidermal flap to seal without water exposure prevents opportunistic bacteria from entering the residual tract, ensuring rapid, painless healing.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review only required if the site becomes inflamed, red, or painful over the next 3 to 5 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department or local GP immediately if the palm becomes intensely red, swollen, hot, starts throbbing severely, or if red lines track up your wrist.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Wood splinters can occasionally break off microscopically; if a hard, painful lump forms at the site in a few weeks, further exploration may be needed.
Clinical Course: The sharp localized pain will resolve almost immediately post-extraction. The small skin defect will close fully over 3 to 5 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical attention if you suddenly cannot straighten your fingers due to pain, or if the palm swells dramatically overnight.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Wood is porous and holds high levels of soil bacteria. Complete removal using sterile instruments ensures no organic material remains to cause an abscess.
Actionable Steps: Wear thick leather gardening gloves for all future yard work. Wash the hand gently with soap and water starting tomorrow. Do not pick at the healing skin flap.

